  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nAugust begins with the false hope of cooler temperatures to come, as \nFriday's cold front should drop high temps in the low to mid 90s \nacross the Panhandles. Global guidance shows the dome of high \npressure shifting slightly westward over the desert southwest during \nthis weekend, allowing a brief pattern shift to northwest flow atop \nthe region. This pattern is typically more favorable for \nprecipitation, but lack of moisture behind the front will limit us \nto a 10-20% chance for some lingering showers/storms on Saturday \nacross the western TX Panhandle. But overall, this weekend should \nhypothetically hold some of the best temperatures over the next 7 \ndays. \n\nAll good things must end though, as most models unfortunately show \nhigh pressure meandering back towards the Plains early next week. \nProbabilities heavily favor the return of triple digit heat as a \nresult, lasting through at least mid next week. Low chances for \nspotty summertime storms will return as well (15-20%), but the Euro \nand GFS are both trending drier compared to this week in terms of \ndeep layer moisture availability. Ensemble mean precipitation \noutlooks are holding fairly steady from run to run, with overall \nunimpressive amounts of rainfall being brought to the region \n(<0.5\" through next week). \n\nHarrel",
